Before running any kubectl command on the Jenkins server, run 
aws configure
aws eks update-kubeconfig --region ap-south-1 --name my-eks-cluster

Now, kubectl commands will be allowed to be run.

Check - kubectl get nodes


Helm installation: Helm installation: 
Refer to the website for the latest information: https://helm.sh/docs/intro/install

curl -fsSL -o get_helm.sh https://raw.githubusercontent.com/helm/helm/main/scripts/get-helm-4
chmod 700 get_helm.sh
./get_helm.sh
helm version


Prometheus and Grafana installation using Helm:
kubectl create namespace devsecops
helm repo add prometheus-community https://prometheus-community.github.io/helm-charts
helm repo update
helm install kube-prometheus-stack prometheus-community/kube-prometheus-stack -namespace devsecops

Command to get the admin username password for Grafana:
kubectl --namespace monitor get secrets kube-prometheus-stack-grafana -o jsonpath="{.data.admin-password}" | base64 -d ; echo

To access Grafana on port 3000:
kubectl expose deployment kube-prometheus-stack-grafana --port=3000 --target-port=3000 --name=grafana --type=LoadBalancer -n devsecops --dry-run=client -o yaml > grafana.yaml

kubectl get all -n devsecops

kubectl get svc -n devsecops
Get the DNS name of the LoadBalancer and type the following in the browser to launch Grafana:
